0 @N1799@ NOTE Other forms used in the 1901 census were: “Form N” (Enumerator’s Ab
1 CONC stract for a Townland or Street), “Form B1” (House and Building Return
1 CONC , which recorded details on the house and general information on the fam
1 CONC ilies residing therein), and “Form B2” (Return of Out-Offices and Farm-S
1 CONC teadings, which listed, with some exceptions, all out-offices intended f
1 CONC or domestic or agricultural purposes).Though national censuses were take
1 CONC n every decade from 1821 to 1911, the 1901 census is the earliest comple
1 CONC te census still in existence; most of the previous censuses were destroy
1 CONC ed.Images and index to this census were created by and obtained from th
1 CONC e National Archives of Ireland.
0 @N1800@ NOTE Civil registration — the government recording of births, marriages
1 CONC , and deaths — began in England and Wales on 1 July 1837. Local registra
1 CONC tion districts had jurisdiction for recording civil events, but were req
1 CONC uired to send copies of their records each quarter to the General Regist
1 CONC er Office (GRO) in London. The GRO created indexes to these records whic
1 CONC h are organized by event, year, and quarter, and thereunder alphabetical
1 CONC ly by surname.Information included in the index changed over the years
1 CONC . The index always provides the name of deceased, registration district
1 CONC , and reference information (volume and page numbers). From 1866 to Marc
1 CONC h 1969, the index also includes age. Beginning in June 1969 the index in
1 CONC cludes the deceased’s birth date rather than age. Information provided i
1 CONC n the index can be used to order a copy of the person’s death certificat
1 CONC e for a fee from the GRO through their Certificate Ordering Service. Dep
1 CONC ending on the year, full death certificates may provide: name of decease
1 CONC d, death date, death place, age, sex, occupation, cause of death, name o
1 CONC f parent if the deceased is a child, informant’s name, residence, and re
1 CONC lationship to the deceased, and date of registration.  Note: Informatio
1 CONC n recorded on a death certificate is only as accurate as the knowledge o
1 CONC f the person reporting it. It is important to pay attention to who the i
1 CONC nformant was and their relationship to the deceased. The closer the rela
1 CONC tionship, the more reliable the information likely is. For years where i
1 CONC mages of the index are available, be sure to consult the image to verif
1 CONC y the information presented to you. Sometimes errors happen during the t
1 CONC ranscription process. For example, a “5” may have inadvertently been tra
1 CONC nscribed as a “3”. Since there is a fee for ordering certificate copie
1 CONC s from the GRO, it is especially important to make sure all reference nu
1 CONC mbers are correct before placing an order. Search tip: If an individua
1 CONC l had multiple given names, sometimes only one or two of these names wa
1 CONC s recorded in the index. In addition, some of the given names may have b
1 CONC een recorded by initials only. If you’re having trouble locating someon
1 CONC e in the index, try searching by any of the individual’s known given nam
1 CONC es, initials, or nicknames.
