0 @N1843@ NOTE When the United States declared war on the German Empire on April 6
1 CONC , 1917 its standing army was comprised of approximately 100,000 men wit
1 CONC h another 115,000 in National Guard units. President Wilson immediatel
1 CONC y directed the Department of War to work to increase the army to a one m
1 CONC illion-man force. However, six weeks after war was declared only 73,00
1 CONC 0 new recruits had volunteered for military service.Military planners an
1 CONC d political leaders had correctly anticipated the general apathy in th
1 CONC e nation for the war effort at its onset and almost as soon as war was d
1 CONC eclared work began in the US Congress to enact updated conscription legi
1 CONC slation. Congress passed the Selective Service Act on May 18, 1917. Thi
1 CONC s act authorized the federal government to raise a national army throug
1 CONC h compulsory enlistment.The initial Selective Service Act required all m
1 CONC en aged 21 to 30 to register. In August 1918, at the request of the Wa
1 CONC r Department, Congress amended the law to expand the age range to includ
1 CONC e all men aged 18 to 45.Three specific registrations were conducted
1 CONC :  * June 5, 1917. This first registration was for all men between the a
1 CONC ges of 21 and 31. * June 5, 1918. The second registration was for thos
1 CONC e who had turned 21 after June 5, 1917 and a supplemental registration i
1 CONC ncluded in the second registration was held on August 24, 1918, for thos
1 CONC e who turned 21 years old after June 5, 1918. * September 12, 1918. Th
1 CONC e third, and final registration was for all men aged 18 through 45 not p
1 CONC reviously enrolled. By the end of the First World War, some 2 million me
1 CONC n had volunteered for military service and 2.8 million other men had bee
1 CONC n drafted. Accordingly, a draft registration does not imply that the ind
1 CONC ividual ended up being drafted or that he didn’t volunteer separately. T
1 CONC he handwriting on the card is normally that of a registration board work
1 CONC er usually labeled the “registrar”. However, almost all cards contain th
1 CONC e signature or “mark” in the handwriting of the registrant himself.
