0 @N1851@ NOTE The 1880 census contains records of families living in the United S
1 CONC tates and its territories during the latter half of the Great Westward M
1 CONC igration. Thirty-eight states were included in the 1880 census, plus th
1 CONC e territories of: Arizona, Dakota, Idaho, Montana, New Mexico, Utah, Was
1 CONC hington, and Wyoming. Non-organized Alaska was also enumerated, but th
1 CONC e "Indian Territory" (now Oklahoma) was not enumerated for non-Indians.F
1 CONC ederal census takers were asked to record information about every perso
1 CONC n who was in each household on the census day. A census taker might hav
1 CONC e visited a house on a later date, but the information he collected wa
1 CONC s supposed to be about the people who were in the house on the census da
1 CONC y. The basic census enumeration unit was the county. Each county was div
1 CONC ided into enumeration districts, one for each enumerator. The complete
1 CONC d forms were sent to the Commerce Department's Census Office in Washingt
1 CONC on, D.C.Federal censuses are usually reliable, depending on the knowledg
1 CONC e of the informant and the care of the census enumerator. Information ma
1 CONC y have been given to a census taker by any member of the family or b
1 CONC y a neighbor. Some information may have been incorrect or deliberately f
1 CONC alsified.
