0 @N1895@ NOTE The decade of the 1940s, leading up to the 1950 Census, was one o
1 CONC f incredible sorrow, extraordinary patriotism, and ultimately hope and t
1 CONC he beginning of a new era of America’s elevated position on the world st
1 CONC age following the conclusion of World War II. This decade left an indeli
1 CONC ble mark on just about all Americans then living and those who came of a
1 CONC ge during the Great Depression and went on to fight in and contribute t
1 CONC o the war effort have become known as The Greatest Generation as popular
1 CONC ized by the journalist Tom Brokaw. Regardless of membership in these var
1 CONC ious demographic generations and their generalizations, these census rec
1 CONC ords capture a unique and detailed snapshot of Americans at the individu
1 CONC al and family level. This index and the associated 6.38 million digita
1 CONC l images of the original handwritten population schedules will be an unp
1 CONC aralleled resource for family historians, genealogists, social scientist
1 CONC s, and other researchers for decades to come.Original data: United State
1 CONC s of America, Bureau of the Census. Seventeenth Census of the United Sta
1 CONC tes, 1950. Washington, D.C.: National Archives and Records Administratio
1 CONC n publication T628, 6,373 rolls.
0 @N1896@ NOTE The records primarily consist of soldiers from the First and Secon
1 CONC d World Wars with a few records from different wars. The number of Briti
1 CONC sh casualties was smaller in wars following World War II, and the numbe
1 CONC r of records from other conflicts is consequently low. This collection c
1 CONC ontent is copyright of the Imperial War Museums and the index is provide
1 CONC d by MyHeritage free of charge as a beneficial service to the genealog
1 CONC y community.
