0 @N1963@ NOTE The collection provides details from an extensive array of burial g
1 CONC rounds, spanning from national to private cemeteries within and beyond t
1 CONC he nation's borders. Encompassing a broad timeline, this collection feat
1 CONC ures veterans from a diverse array of conflicts, including but not limit
1 CONC ed to World Wars, Vietnam, Korea, and various others.
0 @N1964@ NOTE Birth records represent one of the key primary sources for family i
1 CONC nformation, typically being issued within days of a birth and containin
1 CONC g the parents' names at a minimum. Many times they contain additional pa
1 CONC rental details including places of birth, ages, number of children and r
1 CONC eligious affiliation for baptism and christening records.
0 @N1965@ NOTE United States Army involvement in World War II formally began wit
1 CONC h attack on Pearl Harbor December 7, 1941. Germany, Italy, the Soviet Un
1 CONC ion, Japan, and China had already been fighting with the United Kingdom
1 CONC , France, and other forces as early as 1939 with the invasion by German
1 CONC y into Poland.With more than nine million American men and women involve
1 CONC d in the European and Pacific conflicts, there is a high likelihood tha
1 CONC t a relative of someone searching today could have fought in World War I
1 CONC I. These records lead to further information about the person, and to fu
1 CONC rther records concerning pensions, and medical records.After finding per
1 CONC tinent information for a veteran, requesting whatever military records a
1 CONC re available is the next step to receiving pension records, medical reco
1 CONC rds, and other personnel files. Due to a fire at the St. Louis facilit
1 CONC y in 1973, it is less likely to find military records between 1912 and 1
1 CONC 959, but there are possibilities. In order to view original records at t
1 CONC he facility is it highly recommended to make an appointment by calling t
1 CONC he NPRC Archival Research Room in St. Louis, MO: 314-801-0850.The follow
1 CONC ing is from the National Personnel Records Center:The National Personne
1 CONC l Records Center, Military Personnel Records (NPRC-MPR) is the repositor
1 CONC y of millions of military personnel, health, and medical records of disc
1 CONC harged and deceased veterans of all services during the 20th century. (R
1 CONC ecords prior to WWI are in Washington, DC.) NPRC (MPR) also stores medic
1 CONC al treatment records of retirees from all services, as well as records f
1 CONC or dependent and other persons treated at naval medical facilities. Info
1 CONC rmation from the records is made available upon written request (with si
1 CONC gnature and date) to the extent allowed by law.This site is provided fo
1 CONC r those seeking information regarding military personnel, health and med
1 CONC ical records stored at NPRC (MPR).If you are a veteran or next-of-kin o
1 CONC f a deceased veteran, you may now use vetrecs.archives.gov to order a co
1 CONC py of your military records. For all others, your request is best made u
1 CONC sing a Standard Form 180. It includes complete instructions for preparin
1 CONC g and submitting requests. Please Note: All requests must be in writing
1 CONC , signed and mailed to us at the address shown below.National Personne
1 CONC l Records Center Military Personnel Records 9700 Page Avenue St. Louis
1 CONC , MO 63132-5100http://www.archives.gov/st-louis/military-personnel/index
1 CONC .htmlNational Archives and Records Administration. Office of Records Ser
1 CONC vices - Washington, D.C. Modern Records Programs. Electronic and Specia
1 CONC l Media Records Services Division. Series from Record Group 64: Record
1 CONC s of the National Archives and Records Administration. Although this ser
1 CONC ies was created in 2002, it contains information from the period 1938 th
1 CONC rough 1946. The agency converted the Army Serial Number microfilm of com
1 CONC puter punch cards to electronic form to support the military records rec
1 CONC onstruction project. This file resulted from a subsequent project to pre
1 CONC pare the records for use in the Access to Archival Databases project.
