0 @N2004@ NOTE This content comes from an index to marriages by brides and a separ
1 CONC ate index to marriages by grooms. The records published here have, in mo
1 CONC st cases, been joined to unify the information of the brides and the gro
1 CONC oms in their respective marriages. The collection has a small number o
1 CONC f records which could not be matched and joined together. The original i
1 CONC ndexes also contain some duplicate records with variant spellings of giv
1 CONC en names and/or surnames. For example, a groom might have three index re
1 CONC cords for the same marriage where his given name is spelled as “Dominic
1 CONC ”, “Dominick C”, or “Dominic C” and in these cases duplicate records wil
1 CONC l exist in this published collection as they exist in the original index
1 CONC es. The below paragraphs are provided to assist in understanding the inf
1 CONC ormation listed in the “State File No.” and “Place of Marriage” columns
1 CONC , as shown on the index images. Please note that MyHeritage has used thi
1 CONC s information to correctly interpret and display these fields in this co
1 CONC llection.  From 1915 to 1919 the State File Number represents the volum
1 CONC e and certificate number. The first two digits represent the volume numb
1 CONC er. The last three digits represent the certificate number. The certific
1 CONC ate number resets each year. The Place of Marriage code is not listed i
1 CONC n this year range. The original records are available on microfilm at th
1 CONC e New Jersey State Archive and are organized alphabetically by the groom
1 CONC ’s surname and the year of marriage. From 1920 to 1929 the index display
1 CONC s a two digit number which represents the county where the marriage too
1 CONC k place. The original records are available on microfilm at the New Jers
1 CONC ey State Archive and can be requested with the Groom’s surname and the y
1 CONC ear of marriage. From 1930 to 1964 the place code is written as a four t
1 CONC o six-digit number. In order to find the code you truncate the last on
1 CONC e to two digits making this a four-digit number. You can find a key fo
1 CONC r some of these codes here. The original records are available on microf
1 CONC ilm at the New Jersey State Archive. From 1965 to 1984 the place code i
1 CONC s written as a four-digit number. You can find a key for some of these c
1 CONC odes here. The original records are available on microfilm at the New Je
1 CONC rsey State Archive.  From 1985 to current day they stopped using place c
1 CONC odes and started writing the places out in an abbreviated form. The orig
1 CONC inal records are available on microfilm at the New Jersey State Archive
1 CONC . Beginning September 21, 2013 same sex marriage was legalized in New Je
1 CONC rsey. Records included in this collection reflect this change. Marriag
1 CONC e records are an important genealogical record as they not only record i
1 CONC nformation about the bride, the groom, and their residence when the marr
1 CONC iage occurred but often contain additional information such as birth dat
1 CONC es, birthplaces, occupations, and whether single, widowed, or divorced a
1 CONC t the time of the marriage. Often a marriage license will also contain i
1 CONC nformation about the parents of the bride and groom including the parent
1 CONC s’ names and birthplaces. Copies of the original marriage records foun
1 CONC d in this index can be requested from the State Archive. Thanks to Debbi
1 CONC e Hadley, a New Jersey genealogist, for the research provided. You can r
1 CONC each her through her website, bringoutyerdead.com.
